			<div class="content" id="post-content-4847074">
									

<p>Author, you have 400 words to write out your idea between the pitch, narrative and hook. You're 108 words over the limit.</p>
<p>As for the idea itself&#8230; I'm going to have to say <strong>no greenlight</strong>. You've got two or three different ideas (psychic caveman, mysterious organization that wants to somehow bring back old gods using genetic manipulation, same organization inducing atavism) that don't really gel with one another, on top of other problems.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>Why do they need to be psychic? What does it add to the story, other than a compulsion effect? Is this meant to be a sign of some kind of 'divine' aspect, or is it just something humans used to have? If the former, that needs to be made more clear, and if the latter&#8230; well, since this SCP deals with evolution, it has to overcome the question of why humans don't have psychic powers today.</li>
<li>The idea that a humanoid ancestor had a 'lower IQ' is debatable because 1) IQ is a BS measurement that only really measures your ability to complete puzzles and 2) there are different kinds of intelligence; a Neanderthal would probably have been more naturally intelligent (i.e. more able to be an effective hunter-gatherer) than mathematically intelligent, but that doesn't make them any less smart in that particular field.</li>
<li>Evolution is not &quot;always positive&quot;, and what qualifies as &quot;positive&quot; is very much relative; for the bacteria that causes staph infections, becoming antibiotic resistant is a very good thing for it, but a very bad thing for us. A lot of parts of evolution are just mutations that don't get bred out for whatever reason; hair color is an example of this.</li>
<li>Furthermore, &quot;reverting&quot; evolution is likely not going to be &quot;mostly negative&quot;, as evolution adapts to whatever sort of niche that organisms need to fill in that particular epoch. 65 million years ago, theropod dinosaurs were massive things like T. Rex because the niche of a superpredator was appropriate during a time of herbivorous megafauna; as those died out and food became more scarce, nature selected for smaller and smaller theropods that didn't need to eat as much and had the ability to fly, until we're left with everything from kestrels to blue jays in the anthropocene.</li>
<li>Finally: evolution in reality is not evolution in Pokemon. If you took the DNA of a <em>Homo sapiens</em> and tried to &quot;revert&quot; it back to a Neanderthal, the closest you could get is attempting to express genes that cause them to resemble Neanderthals; modern human DNA (outside of African peoples) does contain Neanderthal genes, but it is nowhere near enough to revert them back to a Neanderthal, with most humans outside of Africa only possessing 1-2.5% in their genes. If you were attempting to clone a Neanderthal based on existing DNA from remains (which is theoretically possible, as Neanderthal remains still have intact DNA, but very much an ethical quagmire) then it would be a different story.</li>
<li>I don't see what correlation exists between &quot;ancient creatures and science&quot; and &quot;speaking Latin&quot;.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>

